l absolutely you can talk to the  government this is between  um you know attacking and blaming NHS  staff and directing the blame where it  really is is deserved at the feet of the  government then then I think Mr Streeter  needs to needs to really focus on the  government who are responsible for the  for the problems we face  there have been dire warnings from all  parts of the NHS and and some of the  statistics that have been you know in  the news so much over the last few days  in all of your times working in the NHS  have you ever seen it this bad  no I think I think the honest answer is  no we haven't ever seen it this bad  we've got you know such extreme levels  of vacancy you know about 10 000 doctors  missing in Secretary care alone 47 000  nurses missing we've got 1 900 GPS fewer  than we did in in 2015 and provided more  appointments than ever so 32 million  appointments provided by those GPS this  year and yet we absolutely can't meet  the demand that we have on the service  um and that's a whole mixture of factors  but it means that we can't do as as well  as we would want to for patients and you  know we we hear all of the real patient  stories that underlines that misery for  us and it and it impacts on our mental  health and our well-being as well and  that's why so many are leaving one of  the reasons so many are leaving  if we want to fix those problems we  really have to tackle the issues of pay  pensions and working conditions that are  forcing staff out it's not just about  recruitment at the bottom end it's about  keeping the staff who are already in the  service and providing training to our  younger colleagues  do you think the NHS can cope this  winter  well the NHS is already collapsing and  has been for some some time now so are  we going to see you know worsening of  that collapse as demand increases this  winter and we have another covert wave  we have another flu wave we have and the  strep a both you know situation and the  Panic associated with it but are we  going to see worsening a worsening  situation I'm almost certain of that and  what can the government do things more  people will die  uh I do I do think more people will die  and I don't think  um you know this that's not something  that we that we want and there are some  solutions to that  um but the currently the government  won't even speak to us so we're in a  we're in a really difficult difficult  situation that none of us want to be in  as health workers we've all committed to  providing you know excellent patient  care we're aware that we're not able to  do that at the minute and we're doing  our best you know we're driving  ourselves into the ground trying to  provide that and we're working with each  other and increasingly we're organizing  to take a stand over our service and we  want our profession and you know for  doctors we want our profession to be  valued and and we want our skills across  the Health Service to be valued we want  the government and and patients from the  public to realize that we are trying but  also we're reaching the point where we  have to take action because if we don't  do anything it will keep getting worse  and you're balloting your members for a  pay rise for junior doctors  um what do you think the likelihood is  that they will go out on strike in the  new year  I think it's likely unless the  government is willing to negotiate with  us about pay so we're asking for a  reversal of a pay cut we've had you know  a pay cut for junior doctors of 26.1  since 2008 and so over a quarter of our  pay has been lost and this year again  we've received two percent pay award  which is about 10 less than inflation  that's another month working for free  I'll work in a whole month for free  compared to last year  currently won't even say sorry yeah  forgive me we haven't got a huge amount  of time but but what what number would  you like if you would if your dream  number that the government would give  you  but we're looking for pay restoration so  restoration of that pay cut yeah  absolutely but it's only a lot for the  government will give you a 25 pay rise  well it's it's only as steep as the  money that has been cut from us as only  as steep as the money that we've lost so  if the government as you know the  government has continued to give us  below inflation uh pay Awards year after  year no claps do not pay the bills you  know we are at a point now where we are  you know 10 000 doctors short in  secondary care alone  1900 fewer GPS than we've had in the  past we we really need to tackle the  problems of keeping staff in the service  and whether that's doctors nurses  ambulance staff you know paramedics  radiographers the whole team really need  to understand and to and to be valued by  the government and and therefore value  our patients and the service that we're  providing to them as well so to be clear  on this program you're saying you want a  25 pay increase  okay so if as inflation changes and we  will continue to seek restoration of the  losses that we've had okay  but you're saying that's 25 I mean as  you referred to the Goodwill that that  there was throughout covert and the  Clapping on the doorsteps many in the  country would love to see those in the  medical profession getting a pay rise  but you also said that you think people  will die partly as a result of the  strikes partly as a result of of the  state that the NHS is in over of this  winter do you think that there's going  to be a change are you worried that that  the public will perhaps not be quite as  much in love with the NHS after a few  weeks of strikes as they are now I think  that the public  um really understand  that our desire for investment in health  workers and the Health Service  is fundamentally about the wanting to be  able to provide and a really excellent  standard of care for patients that's  what it's about there's a there's an  absolute link between investment and the  Heal